title: read-only commands render an empty board where there is no board
updated: 2026-08-11T21:50:14Z
---

board, list, show, log and context call neither sync_before nor require_board.
Reading offline is defensible -- they are the fast local surfaces -- but the
consequence is that a repository with no local board is indistinguishable from
a board with nothing on it.

Found while verifying #134, in a fresh clone whose karr refs had never been
fetched:

    $ git clone ssh://.../dbio-clickhouse.git
    $ karr board
    # Kanban Board
    ## Backlog
    ## Todo
    ...
    0 tasks
    $ git for-each-ref refs/karr/** | wc -l
    0

The board is rendered from the default config with an empty task list, so the
answer reads "this board is empty" when the truth is "no board has been loaded
here". A user who trusts it concludes their tickets are gone. Every write
command syncs and would have told them otherwise.

This is the same family as #133: the information to answer correctly is right
there (board_exists / has_board_refs), the command just never asks. Now that
require_board distinguishes an empty repository from a half-board, the read
commands should consult it too -- or, if staying silent is deliberate, say
what was actually read: no board here, nothing fetched yet, run karr sync.

Note the interaction with a fresh clone specifically: refs/karr/* are not
fetched by git clone, so this is the normal state of every newly cloned
repository, not an edge case.
